nodecode-coding-route is a rewrite of metamindedu's metamindedu/hermes-model-router:

  https://github.com/metamindedu/hermes-model-router
  commit e207667489ac8a796d53f875126d6ee227201112
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 NewMetaW (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the two lanes and the six purposes that pick between them,
the background job a route starts, and the reader that answers what the job
said. The original opened a Hermes profile's config, wrote model, delegation,
fallback and skill keys into it through Hermes' own config CLI, and started
detached Hermes processes with a worker script; this cell starts a detached
session of its own in the organism, pins the lane's provider, model and
reasoning effort on it, submits the task as its first turn and reads the answer
back out of the session's log. The route table, the two known lanes, the
purpose-to-lane rule and the four states a job can be in are the original's.

What was left out, and why: the Hermes Desktop page and the multi-profile
settings editor (profiles, config.yaml write-back, backups, CAS and readback)
have no counterpart outside Hermes -- Nodecode has one config, and a session
pins its own model, so the cell declares the two routes in its section
instead. Also left out: native-delegation and conversation fallback chains,
Fast-tier inheritance, per-route total-runtime budgets and the fixed model
catalogue the original shipped, whose ids belong to one provider's September
2026 table rather than to any Nodecode install. The pinned plugin-asset
installer and the Windows shortcut are Hermes packaging, not behaviour.
